WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.580
When I was a graduate student I had no
real conception of the time scale

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.750
involved in math research. For me, in
everything I've done it's been very slow.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:22.140
You get into a field, takes a long time to
learn what's the what's going on. Long

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.099
time to figure out what you should be
working on, a long time to solve it and

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:29.699
then sometimes at that point you you
understand things a bit better you

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:33.960
realize well that wasn't the right thing
to have been working on. So for me that

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.879
that time scale has been five to ten
years in the things I've done and I just

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:47.940
had no conception of that both at length
of time when I was a graduate student. My

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.930
working sounds very collaborative.
I've had many co-authors and I've worked

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:58.609
with them for a long time. For me there
are two really important aspects of that.

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:05.070
One is that in a group usually someone
has an idea so it has much more of a

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:08.840
sense of continuous progress and when
you're by yourself and very often you're

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:14.420
you have longer periods of being stuck
by yourself. The second which I think is

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.500
more important if then is the social
aspect. When you're doing math by

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:23.430
yourself it can be very isolating and
when you work in a group it turns that

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:28.049
individual activity into something
that's much more social and pleasant and

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:35.900
human. So for me that aspect has been
really important to my career.

